 "cells": [
  {
   "id": "crush-capabilities-md-1",
   "kind": {
    "type": "markdown"
   },
   "source": "# Capability System\n\nCrush programs can't touch the outside world directly. Every interaction with the\nhost — printing, reading a file, making an HTTP request, spawning a process,\nrunning another language — is a **capability call**, and a capability only exists\nif the host that is running the program has explicitly turned it on. There is no\nambient authority.\n\n> **Status: alpha.** The model below is real and enforced, but the set of\n> capabilities is small and the permission granularity is coarse (on/off per\n> group, with a directory root for the filesystem). Read\n> [What is not enforced](#what-is-not-enforced) before relying on it for\n> isolation.\n\n## Calling a capability\n\nA capability call looks like an ordinary dotted function call — **no `@` prefix**:\n",
   "state": {
    "status": "done"
   },
   "meta": {},
   "outputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": "crush-capabilities-1",
   "kind": {
    "type": "crush"
   },
   "source": "io.print(\"Hello, \", \"Crush\")\nlet upper = str.concat(\"a\", \"b\")\nprint(upper)\n",
   "state": {
    "status": "pending"
   },
   "meta": {
    "tags": [
     "guide",
     "runnable"
    ],
    "extra": {
     "guide": {
      "src": "crush/capabilities.md:19",
      "browser": "run",
      "expect_output": "Hello, Crush\nab\n"
     }
    }
   },
   "outputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": "crush-capabilities-md-3",
   "kind": {
    "type": "markdown"
   },
   "source": "`io.print(a, b, ...)` takes any number of arguments, joins them with no\nseparator, and appends a newline. The built-in `print(x)` takes exactly one.\n(The `@` sigil exists in Crush, but it introduces polyglot blocks and\nannotations, never capability calls — see [Syntax](https://docs.crushlang.org/crush/syntax.html#language-blocks).)\n\n## Always available\n\nThese work with no flags:\n\n| Capability | Description |\n|---|---|\n| `io.print(...)` | print to stdout |\n| `io.read()` | read one line from stdin (empty string at EOF) |\n| `str.concat(...)`, `str.len(s)` | concatenate / byte length |\n| `conv.chr(n)`, `conv.ord(s)` | codepoint ↔ one-character string |\n",
   "state": {
    "status": "done"
   },
   "meta": {},
   "outputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": "crush-capabilities-2",
   "kind": {
    "type": "crush"
   },
   "source": "print(conv.chr(65))\nprint(conv.ord(\"A\"))\nprint(str.len(\"héllo\"))\n",
   "state": {
    "status": "pending"
   },
   "meta": {
    "tags": [
     "guide",
     "runnable"
    ],
    "extra": {
     "guide": {
      "src": "crush/capabilities.md:47",
      "browser": "run",
      "expect_output": "A\n65\n6\n"
     }
    }
   },
   "outputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": "crush-capabilities-md-5",
   "kind": {
    "type": "markdown"
   },
   "source": "`io.read` reads from standard input:\n\n```crush\nlet name = io.read()\nio.print(\"Hello, \" + name)\n```\n\n## Enabling more capabilities\n\nWith the CLI, each *group* of capabilities is switched on by a flag on\n`crush-run run` (or `crush run`). Anything not switched on is simply **not\nregistered**, and calling it fails at run time with `unknown capability`:\n\n| Flag | Capabilities enabled |\n|---|---|\n| `--stdlib` | `str.*`, `math.*`, `conv.*`, `collections.*`, `json.*`, `path.*`, `regex.*`, `bytes.*`, `buffer.*`, `binary.*`, `result.*`, `time.format/parse`, `env.os/arch`, `system.*` — pure computation, see [Standard Library](https://docs.crushlang.org/crush/stdlib.html) |\n| `--fs` | `fs.read`, `fs.write`, `fs.exists`, `fs.list`, `text.head/tail/wc/cut/grep` |\n| `--env` | `env.get` |\n| `--time` | `time.now`, `time.now_ms`, `time.now_iso`, `time.elapsed`, `time.sleep` |\n| `--net` | `net.http_get`, `net.http_post` |\n| `--process` | `process.exec` |\n| `--crypto` | `crypto.sha256`, `crypto.random` |\n| `--db PATH` | `db.query`, `db.execute` on that SQLite file |\n| `--graphics` | `graphics.canvas/rect/circle/text/to_svg` |\n| `--bus` | `message_bus.publish/subscribe/recv` |\n| `--task` | `task.start/stop/list` |\n| `--akg` | `akg.write/read/search` |\n| `--polyglot` | `@python { }`, `@javascript { }`, `@bash { }` blocks — see [Polyglot](https://docs.crushlang.org/crush/polyglot.html) |\n\n`crush-run caps` prints the list for your build. Some groups (`--stdlib`,\n`--net`, `--db`, `--graphics`) are Cargo features of `crush-lang-sdk`; if your binary was\nbuilt without one, its flag prints `warning: --… requires the '…' feature (not\nenabled in this build)` and the group stays absent. Note that **`stdlib` is off by\ndefault** (crush-ast CRUSH-113); build with `--features stdlib`.\n\nWithout the flag, the call fails:\n",
   "state": {
    "status": "done"
   },
   "meta": {},
   "outputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": "crush-capabilities-3",
   "kind": {
    "type": "crush"
   },
   "source": "fn main() {\n    print(fs.read(\"data.txt\"))\n}\n",
   "state": {
    "status": "pending"
