  {
   "id": "crush-capabilities-md-9",
   "kind": {
    "type": "markdown"
   },
   "source": "Filesystem paths must be relative and resolve **inside** `--fs-root` (default `.`):\nabsolute paths and `..` escapes are rejected. This is the one place the permission\nhas a scope.\n\n### Filesystem\n",
   "state": {
    "status": "done"
   },
   "meta": {},
   "outputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": "crush-capabilities-5",
   "kind": {
    "type": "crush"
   },
   "source": "print(fs.exists(\"missing.txt\"))\n",
   "state": {
    "status": "pending"
   },
   "meta": {
    "tags": [
     "guide",
     "needs-host"
    ],
    "extra": {
     "guide": {
      "src": "crush/capabilities.md:119",
      "browser": "host",
      "why": "uses `fs.exists`, which the in-browser runtime does not provide",
      "expect_output": "0\n"
     }
    }
   },
   "outputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": "crush-capabilities-md-11",
   "kind": {
    "type": "markdown"
   },
   "source": "| Capability | Arguments | Returns |\n|---|---|---|\n| `fs.read` | `path` | file contents (string) |\n| `fs.exists` | `path` | `1` / `0` |\n| `fs.list` | `dir` | array of names |\n| `fs.write` | `path, data` | nothing — **see the note below** |\n\n> **Known bug — `fs.write`.** It declares no return value, and the compiler emits\n> a `pop` after a call used as a statement, so any program that calls it ends with\n> `[runtime] stack underflow` (the write itself may still happen). Treat `fs.write`\n> as not usable from `.crush` source until this is fixed. Capabilities that\n> return nothing share the problem; `io.print` and `time.sleep` are unaffected.\n",
   "state": {
    "status": "done"
   },
   "meta": {},
   "outputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": "crush-capabilities-6",
   "kind": {
    "type": "crush"
   },
   "source": "fs.write(\"out.txt\", \"hello\")\nprint(fs.read(\"out.txt\"))\n",
   "state": {
    "status": "pending"
   },
